WEBVTT

00:00.150 --> 00:00.983
Instructor: In this video,

00:00.983 --> 00:02.700
we'll cover the git add command

00:02.700 --> 00:04.500
which is the first of two commands

00:04.500 --> 00:06.540
that we'll use to make a commit.

00:06.540 --> 00:09.600
And as we previously talked about in the last video,

00:09.600 --> 00:10.980
we use two commands

00:10.980 --> 00:11.940
because that allows us

00:11.940 --> 00:15.420
to separate out particular changes we've made.

00:15.420 --> 00:18.660
We can, I think I used the imagery of tweezers,

00:18.660 --> 00:21.300
so we can tweeze them out and select them

00:21.300 --> 00:23.190
before we make a commit

00:23.190 --> 00:25.020
so we can group things together.

00:25.020 --> 00:28.350
And this git add command is what enables that to happen.

00:28.350 --> 00:30.030
So once we've done some work,

00:30.030 --> 00:31.680
we've actually made changes,

00:31.680 --> 00:33.930
we then call those changes out

00:33.930 --> 00:35.970
using the git add command

00:35.970 --> 00:38.040
to stage them for a commit

00:38.040 --> 00:39.540
and then we can make a commit.

00:39.540 --> 00:42.120
And in this video, we're focusing on the first piece.

00:42.120 --> 00:44.760
And before we can do anything with Git,

00:44.760 --> 00:45.990
we need to make some changes.

00:45.990 --> 00:49.500
We need to do that first part, work on stuff.

00:49.500 --> 00:51.750
So I'm gonna do a quick demo.

00:51.750 --> 00:54.085
I made this folder, MyFirstNovel.

00:54.085 --> 00:55.980
I'm pretending I'm writing a novel.

00:55.980 --> 00:57.480
We have a Git repository in here,

00:57.480 --> 00:59.820
as we did, we initialized it earlier.

00:59.820 --> 01:02.010
So I'm now pretending to sit down,

01:02.010 --> 01:03.540
it's day one of writing my novel

01:03.540 --> 01:05.160
and I'm gonna do some work.

01:05.160 --> 01:07.050
And if I was actually writing a novel,

01:07.050 --> 01:09.360
I would probably start by making an outline.

01:09.360 --> 01:10.890
That's just how I like to work.

01:10.890 --> 01:12.570
I outline all my courses.

01:12.570 --> 01:15.873
So I just made a new file there, outline.txt.

01:16.890 --> 01:19.770
And I've opened this folder up in a text editor.

01:19.770 --> 01:21.390
I'm using VS Code.

01:21.390 --> 01:23.790
So here it is, it's just an empty file.

01:23.790 --> 01:25.170
So if I'm writing my novel,

01:25.170 --> 01:26.880
I'll put some basic stuff in here.

01:26.880 --> 01:29.550
I am pretending that I'm writing 'The Great Gatsby'

01:29.550 --> 01:31.170
just so we're upfront about that.

01:31.170 --> 01:33.540
It's now in the public domain in 2021.

01:33.540 --> 01:36.000
So yep, I'm writing 'The Great Gatsby'.

01:36.000 --> 01:38.520
So I'm going to be writing part of chapter one

01:38.520 --> 01:40.080
so I start the outline.

01:40.080 --> 01:41.190
All I know so far,

01:41.190 --> 01:43.110
it's very early on in the writing process,

01:43.110 --> 01:45.180
is that we're gonna meet some of our characters

01:45.180 --> 01:46.013
in chapter one.

01:46.013 --> 01:48.330
I haven't figured out what else happens yet

01:48.330 --> 01:50.490
so I'll save that file.

01:50.490 --> 01:54.030
And if I come over here and I type git status,

01:54.030 --> 01:57.300
we now have made some changes in this project

01:57.300 --> 01:58.710
and Git notices something,

01:58.710 --> 02:01.020
it says something it didn't say earlier.

02:01.020 --> 02:04.350
Untracked files, outline.txt.

02:04.350 --> 02:05.183
Hmm.

02:05.183 --> 02:06.690
All right, so I'm still writing,

02:06.690 --> 02:09.180
it's a little bit later in the morning.

02:09.180 --> 02:11.730
I've outlined, you know, I need to meet these characters

02:11.730 --> 02:13.320
or introduce them in chapter one.

02:13.320 --> 02:14.400
Maybe I wanna start

02:14.400 --> 02:17.340
by actually fleshing out those characters.

02:17.340 --> 02:20.610
I'm gonna make a file called characters.txt.

02:20.610 --> 02:22.800
So before I actually write chapter one,

02:22.800 --> 02:24.900
I want to make sure I've hammered out the details

02:24.900 --> 02:26.040
about those characters.

02:26.040 --> 02:26.880
And I'm just gonna copy

02:26.880 --> 02:28.620
and paste some stuff in I've written.

02:28.620 --> 02:30.720
Okay, so we've got Jay Gatsby,

02:30.720 --> 02:33.390
Nick Carraway, Daisy Buchanan, Tom Buchanan.

02:33.390 --> 02:36.270
Just little bios about each character.

02:36.270 --> 02:37.260
And if you're following along,

02:37.260 --> 02:38.970
you can put whatever you want in here.

02:38.970 --> 02:41.370
And then I save the file, all right?

02:41.370 --> 02:43.050
So I'm done for the morning.

02:43.050 --> 02:45.030
I want to take a break

02:45.030 --> 02:47.640
before I actually begin writing chapter one.

02:47.640 --> 02:49.320
So I've saved those files

02:49.320 --> 02:50.970
and if I head back over to my terminal

02:50.970 --> 02:53.160
and I type git status again,

02:53.160 --> 02:55.260
now Git tells me there's two files

02:55.260 --> 02:56.550
that it's aware of,

02:56.550 --> 02:59.400
but it's not tracking, okay?

02:59.400 --> 03:02.370
So we've done step one, I've worked on stuff.

03:02.370 --> 03:05.010
Now I wanna make a commit with what I've done.

03:05.010 --> 03:08.610
Well, now we need to talk about a tiny bit of terminology.

03:08.610 --> 03:10.380
Three different locations,

03:10.380 --> 03:12.240
they're not actual physical locations,

03:12.240 --> 03:14.610
working directory, staging area,

03:14.610 --> 03:16.860
and the repository.

03:16.860 --> 03:18.060
Let's start with this first one,

03:18.060 --> 03:19.440
working directory.

03:19.440 --> 03:22.530
That's just a fancy way of referring to the directory,

03:22.530 --> 03:25.530
the place we're actually working on our project.

03:25.530 --> 03:30.150
So for me, my working directory is this MyFirstNovel folder

03:30.150 --> 03:31.800
and in our working directory,

03:31.800 --> 03:33.660
I have two files at the moment.

03:33.660 --> 03:34.590
I might have more,

03:34.590 --> 03:36.390
I could have 100 files in here.

03:36.390 --> 03:37.740
The working directory just refers

03:37.740 --> 03:40.560
to this directory where I'm working.

03:40.560 --> 03:43.470
Then we have the repository itself.

03:43.470 --> 03:46.170
This is the .git folder.

03:46.170 --> 03:49.860
Remember there is a .git folder here, ls -a.

03:49.860 --> 03:50.940
There it is.

03:50.940 --> 03:52.740
Inside of that folder at the moment,

03:52.740 --> 03:54.510
there's some stuff that's just there,

03:54.510 --> 03:56.250
some default contents,

03:56.250 --> 03:58.500
but once they actually make a commit,

03:58.500 --> 04:01.140
git commit changes what is in .git.

04:01.140 --> 04:04.650
It actually adds new commits to that folder.

04:04.650 --> 04:06.300
So when we actually make a commit,

04:06.300 --> 04:08.220
we are updating that folder,

04:08.220 --> 04:10.590
the actual Git repository.

04:10.590 --> 04:12.110
Otherwise everything I've done so far

04:12.110 --> 04:14.010
is just in the working directory.

04:14.010 --> 04:17.250
And then there's this intermediate thing, this zone,

04:17.250 --> 04:20.370
again, not a physical location, the staging area.

04:20.370 --> 04:22.350
And this is where we add our changes to

04:22.350 --> 04:24.210
before we make a commit.

04:24.210 --> 04:26.640
So here's another flavor of diagram.

04:26.640 --> 04:27.870
We have those three terms,

04:27.870 --> 04:30.870
working directory, staging area, and repository.

04:30.870 --> 04:33.000
On the left side in the working directory,

04:33.000 --> 04:35.460
you can see a bunch of changes that I've made.

04:35.460 --> 04:37.950
So hypothetically, I've been working for a while.

04:37.950 --> 04:40.800
I've done some, you know, I'm working on a project,

04:40.800 --> 04:42.840
some web development project

04:42.840 --> 04:45.510
and I've changed seven files

04:45.510 --> 04:47.640
and Git has noticed that.

04:47.640 --> 04:49.200
So I've decided, okay,

04:49.200 --> 04:52.650
there's actually two discreet things I was working on

04:52.650 --> 04:54.870
and I'm going to make my first commit

04:54.870 --> 04:57.420
with those bottom four files that I changed.

04:57.420 --> 04:59.340
They have to do with the nav bar.

04:59.340 --> 05:00.630
I updated the nav bar

05:00.630 --> 05:03.090
or I created a new nav bar on our webpage.

05:03.090 --> 05:06.180
So I stage those changes first

05:06.180 --> 05:09.000
and I do that using the git add command.

05:09.000 --> 05:11.250
Then I can make a commit.

05:11.250 --> 05:13.620
And then same thing for these other changes.

05:13.620 --> 05:15.090
I can stage them.

05:15.090 --> 05:16.530
Then I make a commit

05:16.530 --> 05:20.160
at which point I'm actually updating the .git folder.

05:20.160 --> 05:21.870
So I know I'm kind of beating a dead horse,

05:21.870 --> 05:23.340
but this is really important.

05:23.340 --> 05:27.030
Two commands, git add is just going to stage changes

05:27.030 --> 05:29.010
so that I then commit them.

05:29.010 --> 05:31.650
And this git add command is pretty simple.

05:31.650 --> 05:34.050
It's git, space, add

05:34.050 --> 05:36.630
and then one or more files,

05:36.630 --> 05:39.120
the name of a file that we've changed.

05:39.120 --> 05:40.830
And we can do more, we can do multiple

05:40.830 --> 05:43.050
if we separate them by spaces.

05:43.050 --> 05:46.110
Otherwise we can just do git add file name.

05:46.110 --> 05:49.320
So over here in my working directory,

05:49.320 --> 05:51.630
I have these two files, right?

05:51.630 --> 05:54.720
I've changed them, they exist.

05:54.720 --> 05:57.660
They didn't exist earlier and Git noticed

05:57.660 --> 05:58.950
and it tells me this.

05:58.950 --> 06:00.270
Let me make this full screen here.

06:00.270 --> 06:02.640
It says untracked files,

06:02.640 --> 06:05.040
characters and outline.txt,

06:05.040 --> 06:06.960
use git add file

06:06.960 --> 06:09.060
to include in what will be committed.

06:09.060 --> 06:11.010
Okay, so why don't we do that?

06:11.010 --> 06:12.570
I'll add the first one.

06:12.570 --> 06:16.320
So git add, and I can do auto complete

06:16.320 --> 06:18.630
if I type something and then hit Tab.

06:18.630 --> 06:21.390
So git add characters.txt.

06:21.390 --> 06:23.133
Now let's type git status again.

06:24.030 --> 06:25.530
I haven't made a commit.

06:25.530 --> 06:28.530
All that I've done is stage the one file,

06:28.530 --> 06:32.040
characters.txt to be committed,

06:32.040 --> 06:34.290
but it's not yet committed.

06:34.290 --> 06:35.850
So if I wanted to,

06:35.850 --> 06:36.990
I could make a commit now

06:36.990 --> 06:39.180
with just that one file that I changed,

06:39.180 --> 06:40.980
but I'm gonna group them together

06:40.980 --> 06:43.170
because you know, I've worked on them together,

06:43.170 --> 06:45.000
they have to do with one another.

06:45.000 --> 06:46.710
Unlike this example here

06:46.710 --> 06:48.150
where I wanted to group things

06:48.150 --> 06:50.430
into two individual commits,

06:50.430 --> 06:53.670
I instead want to group everything on this project so far,

06:53.670 --> 06:55.740
just two files into one commit.

06:55.740 --> 06:59.790
So I'm gonna also add outline.txt

06:59.790 --> 07:01.620
and then I'm gonna look at git status.

07:01.620 --> 07:02.940
We always use git status.

07:02.940 --> 07:04.170
It can never hurt anything.

07:04.170 --> 07:05.700
It just reports back.

07:05.700 --> 07:08.130
And you'll see whenever we make a commit,

07:08.130 --> 07:10.590
those two files will be included.

07:10.590 --> 07:13.470
So what we need to do next is cover git commit.

07:13.470 --> 07:15.620
We need to learn how to make those commits.
